WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.624 --> 00:00:03.131
From wire to pins.

2
00:00:03.131 --> 00:00:05.732
Lecture three of the Manufacturing module.

3
00:00:06.744 --> 00:00:10.111
Lecture two designed our winding pin by pin.

4
00:00:10.111 --> 00:00:15.553
Our EV-size stator, 48 slots and 8 poles, needs 96 hairpins in six shapes.

5
00:00:15.553 --> 00:00:21.814
Now we make them, from a reel of flat copper wire to a formed

6
00:00:21.814 --> 00:00:23.364
pin, ready to insert.

7
00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:26.204
Every pin goes through the same steps:

8
00:00:26.204 --> 00:00:30.793
straighten, strip, cut, bend the U, form the crown.

9
00:00:30.793 --> 00:00:36.173
One machine maker does them all in one cell, at one to one and

10
00:00:36.173 --> 00:00:38.047
a half seconds a pin:

11
00:00:38.047 --> 00:00:39.812
about two minutes for our stator.

12
00:00:40.984 --> 00:00:45.879
The shapes and lengths come from our Hairpin Motor Designer, the generator that built

13
00:00:45.879 --> 00:00:47.460
the stator in lecture two.

14
00:00:48.562 --> 00:00:49.806
Chapter one.

15
00:00:49.806 --> 00:00:51.230
Flat wire and enamel.

16
00:00:52.814 --> 00:00:56.061
The wire is pure copper, tough-pitch or oxygen-free.

17
00:00:56.061 --> 00:01:02.861
It is drawn, rolled or extruded into a rounded rectangle, then annealed soft, so

18
00:01:02.861 --> 00:01:04.314
it bends without cracking.

19
00:01:05.166 --> 00:01:11.758
Our EV-size pin uses a bar 3.6 millimetres wide and 4.45 high, over the

20
00:01:11.758 --> 00:01:12.558
enamel.

21
00:01:12.157 --> 00:01:18.682
Hairpin wires run from about 2 by 1.5 to 6 by 6 millimetres.

22
00:01:19.566 --> 00:01:25.525
The enamel is built up in many thin passes, often polyamide-imide over a polyester-imide

23
00:01:25.525 --> 00:01:26.325
base.

24
00:01:25.902 --> 00:01:29.790
Our model gives it 0.12 millimetres a side.

25
00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:34.938
That makes 12 percent of the bar's section insulation, not copper.

26
00:01:35.982 --> 00:01:42.635
For 800-volt motors some makers use extruded PEEK instead, a plastic put on in

27
00:01:42.635 --> 00:01:44.044
one pass;

28
00:01:44.044 --> 00:01:47.130
one supplier quotes 0.1 to 0.2 millimetres.

29
00:01:48.046 --> 00:01:52.961
The corners are rounded to radii set in the wire standard, IEC 60317.

30
00:01:52.961 --> 00:01:59.221
A sharp corner concentrates the electric field and is the first place the film

31
00:01:59.221 --> 00:02:01.011
cracks when bent.

32
00:02:01.011 --> 00:02:03.194
A large radius wastes slot copper.

33
00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:07.156
And the wire is not uniform.

34
00:02:07.156 --> 00:02:13.605
One study measured its stiffness, the Young's modulus, at 84 to 98 gigapascals, against

35
00:02:13.605 --> 00:02:18.324
110 to 130 in handbooks, and about ten percent between batches.

36
00:02:18.324 --> 00:02:21.338
Chapter five shows why that matters.

37
00:02:22.399 --> 00:02:23.784
Chapter two.

38
00:02:23.784 --> 00:02:25.579
Straighten, strip, cut.

39
00:02:27.291 --> 00:02:31.082
The wire comes off a reel, so it is curved.

40
00:02:31.082 --> 00:02:35.772
Two sets of rollers, one for each plane, bend it back and forth, less

41
00:02:35.772 --> 00:02:37.671
each time, until it runs straight.

42
00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:41.170
Setting those rollers is a skill.

43
00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:46.379
One machine maker says it can take an experienced operator two hours, so new

44
00:02:46.379 --> 00:02:49.639
lines measure the wire and set the rollers themselves.

45
00:02:50.779 --> 00:02:54.679
Next the enamel comes off where the welds will be:

46
00:02:54.679 --> 00:02:59.646
scraped by a mill or brushes, or burned off by a laser.

47
00:02:59.646 --> 00:03:02.072
One common recipe uses two lasers.

48
00:03:02.072 --> 00:03:06.946
A carbon-dioxide laser takes the bulk but leaves a film of one or two

49
00:03:06.946 --> 00:03:09.383
micrometres, and a second laser cleans it.

50
00:03:10.363 --> 00:03:12.043
Why so clean?

51
00:03:12.043 --> 00:03:13.476
The weld melts the copper.

52
00:03:13.476 --> 00:03:18.721
Any polymer left on it burns, and the gas throws out spatter and leaves

53
00:03:18.721 --> 00:03:19.867
pores in the weld.

54
00:03:19.867 --> 00:03:22.439
Lecture four comes back to this.

55
00:03:23.323 --> 00:03:26.840
In our model each end is bare for nine millimetres:

56
00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:30.023
the seven-millimetre weld tip and two below it.

57
00:03:31.035 --> 00:03:35.699
Then a punch cuts the wire in the middle of a stripped window.

58
00:03:35.699 --> 00:03:40.131
One 18-millimetre window gives the bare end of one pin and the start of

59
00:03:40.131 --> 00:03:41.602
the next.

60
00:03:41.602 --> 00:03:46.258
The punch also chamfers all four edges, so the leg slides into its slot

61
00:03:46.258 --> 00:03:47.431
without cutting the liner.

62
00:03:48.443 --> 00:03:51.131
Each shape has its own length.

63
00:03:51.131 --> 00:03:57.202
In our stator the cut lengths run from 449 millimetres, for the short special

64
00:03:57.202 --> 00:04:01.020
pin, to 492 for the long one.

65
00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:05.543
In all, 45.1 metres of wire and about 5.7 kilograms of copper.

66
00:04:10.785 --> 00:04:11.585
Chapter three.

67
00:04:11.409 --> 00:04:12.877
Bend the U.

68
00:04:14.653 --> 00:04:19.142
First the rod is bent flat, in one plane, into the U you met

69
00:04:19.142 --> 00:04:20.582
in lecture two:

70
00:04:20.582 --> 00:04:23.113
two straight legs and a pointed roof between them.

71
00:04:24.093 --> 00:04:27.100
On our pin the roof rises at 43 degrees.

72
00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:32.619
So each leg turns 47 degrees into the roof, at the shoulder, and the

73
00:04:32.619 --> 00:04:34.868
roof turns 86 degrees at its peak.

74
00:04:34.868 --> 00:04:39.273
The legs end up five slots apart, the crown pitch.

75
00:04:40.189 --> 00:04:42.072
Machines bend it in three ways.

76
00:04:42.072 --> 00:04:46.216
A die presses the whole U in one stroke.

77
00:04:46.216 --> 00:04:49.950
A rotary draw bender makes the bends one after another.

78
00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:54.831
Or a CNC or robot bender moves freely, with no tool tied to one

79
00:04:54.831 --> 00:04:55.631
shape.

80
00:04:56.253 --> 00:05:00.014
Dies are fast, but each shape needs its own:

81
00:05:00.014 --> 00:05:02.271
six for our stator.

82
00:05:02.271 --> 00:05:06.889
Free bending changes the shape in software, but it feels the wire's spread more.

83
00:05:07.773 --> 00:05:09.638
Every bend stretches its outer edge.

84
00:05:09.638 --> 00:05:15.492
The strain is t, the thickness in the bend plane, over twice the inner

85
00:05:15.492 --> 00:05:16.425
radius plus t.

86
00:05:17.469 --> 00:05:22.738
Our shoulder bends lie in the plane of the 3.6-millimetre side, so the bar

87
00:05:22.738 --> 00:05:25.668
bends flat, the easy way.

88
00:05:25.668 --> 00:05:31.181
Round an inner radius of twice that, 7.2 millimetres, the outer edge stretches 20

89
00:05:31.181 --> 00:05:31.981
percent.

90
00:05:32.349 --> 00:05:34.444
The copper takes that easily;

91
00:05:34.444 --> 00:05:37.372
one supplier's flat wire breaks only beyond 32 percent.

92
00:05:37.372 --> 00:05:39.691
The enamel is the limit.

93
00:05:39.691 --> 00:05:45.131
The same data sheet bends the wire edgewise round a mandrel four times its

94
00:05:45.131 --> 00:05:47.822
width, with no cracks allowed:

95
00:05:47.822 --> 00:05:49.449
the same 20 percent stretch.

96
00:05:50.493 --> 00:05:52.075
Does forming harm the enamel?

97
00:05:52.075 --> 00:05:57.293
One study bent edgewise wire to 3.5 millimetres without cracks, and the voltage at

98
00:05:57.293 --> 00:05:59.848
which partial discharges start did not fall.

99
00:05:59.848 --> 00:06:05.406
One patent reports a 35 percent drop at the tight bend in the middle

100
00:06:05.406 --> 00:06:08.754
of the crown, bent and pressed in a die.

101
00:06:08.754 --> 00:06:14.672
So the risk sits in tight, pressed bends, and formed pins are tested, in

102
00:06:14.672 --> 00:06:15.472
lecture five.

103
00:06:16.407 --> 00:06:17.207
Chapter four.

104
00:06:17.082 --> 00:06:18.531
Form the crown.

105
00:06:19.955 --> 00:06:22.669
A flat U does not fit the stator.

106
00:06:22.669 --> 00:06:26.620
Its legs must go into two different layers, and its crown must follow the

107
00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:27.679
curve of the bore.

108
00:06:28.435 --> 00:06:30.705
So a second die forms it in 3D.

109
00:06:30.705 --> 00:06:36.177
At the peak it steps the pin out by one layer pitch, 6 millimetres

110
00:06:36.177 --> 00:06:37.028
on our core.

111
00:06:37.028 --> 00:06:39.583
This step is the layer change.

112
00:06:40.115 --> 00:06:44.062
It also rolls the U onto a cylinder.

113
00:06:44.062 --> 00:06:46.933
Our pin spans 37.5 degrees of the stator.

114
00:06:46.933 --> 00:06:52.009
Left flat, its middle would sit 4.2 millimetres inside the curve.

115
00:06:52.009 --> 00:06:58.853
On a small 24-slot, 4-pole core the span is a quarter turn and the

116
00:06:58.853 --> 00:07:00.558
gap 14 millimetres:

117
00:07:00.558 --> 00:07:03.327
fewer poles, wider pins, more curve.

118
00:07:04.531 --> 00:07:06.221
Lines do this in different orders:

119
00:07:06.221 --> 00:07:11.472
a 2D bend, then a 3D die, sometimes while a robot carries the pin

120
00:07:11.472 --> 00:07:12.317
to the stator;

121
00:07:12.317 --> 00:07:15.199
or the full 3D shape in one machine.

122
00:07:16.179 --> 00:07:18.094
Real bend radii cost height.

123
00:07:18.094 --> 00:07:24.649
Lecture two drew our crown about 38 millimetres high, with tight corners.

124
00:07:24.649 --> 00:07:29.736
With the shoulders bent at 7.2 millimetres, it grows to 44.

125
00:07:29.736 --> 00:07:31.612
The roof stays tight:

126
00:07:31.612 --> 00:07:35.551
the neighbouring pins cross right over it, 1.5 millimetres clear.

127
00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:41.763
Chapter five.

128
00:07:41.763 --> 00:07:43.160
Springback and checks.

129
00:07:44.744 --> 00:07:48.716
When the die opens, the pin opens a little too.

130
00:07:48.716 --> 00:07:53.666
Bending leaves elastic strain in the copper, and when the tool lets go, that

131
00:07:53.666 --> 00:07:54.766
part recovers.

132
00:07:54.766 --> 00:07:55.668
This is springback.

133
00:07:56.520 --> 00:08:02.287
We calculated it for our 47-degree shoulder bend at 7.2 millimetres, using the flow

134
00:08:02.287 --> 00:08:07.098
curve that Wirth and colleagues measured on hairpin wire in 2024:

135
00:08:07.098 --> 00:08:11.510
how its strength rises as it is bent.

136
00:08:11.510 --> 00:08:13.781
It springs back about 0.9 degrees.

137
00:08:13.781 --> 00:08:20.484
That sounds small, but the leg tip is about 200 millimetres away, so it

138
00:08:20.484 --> 00:08:21.620
moves 3.2 millimetres.

139
00:08:22.344 --> 00:08:26.745
So the machine overbends by the amount the wire will spring back.

140
00:08:26.745 --> 00:08:29.455
But that amount moves with the wire.

141
00:08:29.455 --> 00:08:34.361
Make it ten percent less stiff and ten percent harder, and our bend opens

142
00:08:34.361 --> 00:08:35.813
0.2 degrees more:

143
00:08:35.813 --> 00:08:37.652
another 0.7 millimetres at the tip.

144
00:08:38.696 --> 00:08:41.871
A real pin has several bends, and the errors add.

145
00:08:41.871 --> 00:08:47.443
In one simulation, a ten percent spread in the wire moved a pin's open

146
00:08:47.443 --> 00:08:49.690
end by 5.5 to 11.5 millimetres;

147
00:08:49.690 --> 00:08:51.156
the tolerance was under one.

148
00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:57.155
So the best lines close the loop:

149
00:08:57.155 --> 00:09:03.171
measure each pin, compare it with the target, correct the next one.

150
00:09:03.171 --> 00:09:08.313
In one study the first pins, set by a model, were off by 0.6

151
00:09:08.313 --> 00:09:09.753
to 0.8 degrees;

152
00:09:09.753 --> 00:09:12.372
after one correction, by 0.13 at most.

153
00:09:13.256 --> 00:09:14.056
The checks:

154
00:09:13.821 --> 00:09:19.264
a camera in the machine for the leg positions and the stripped ends, with

155
00:09:19.264 --> 00:09:21.337
no enamel left on them;

156
00:09:21.337 --> 00:09:25.567
a 3D scanner offline, about eight minutes a pin in that study;

157
00:09:25.567 --> 00:09:27.828
and the enamel thickness on the incoming wire.

158
00:09:28.712 --> 00:09:29.512
Why so strict?

159
00:09:29.487 --> 00:09:34.882
A pin with a wrong pitch, a bent leg or a misshapen crown jams

160
00:09:34.882 --> 00:09:39.180
the insertion, and once it is in the stator there is no economic way

161
00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:39.980
to fix it.

162
00:09:45.149 --> 00:09:45.949
Summary.

163
00:09:45.541 --> 00:09:46.793
Four points.

164
00:09:47.897 --> 00:09:48.697
One.

165
00:09:48.212 --> 00:09:50.659
The wire sets the start:

166
00:09:50.659 --> 00:09:55.525
soft copper, a rounded rectangle, and enamel that takes 12 percent of our bar.

167
00:09:56.345 --> 00:09:57.145
Two.

168
00:09:56.642 --> 00:09:59.861
Straighten, strip and cut while the bar is straight.

169
00:09:59.861 --> 00:10:05.189
One stripped window, cut in the middle, gives the bare ends of two pins.

170
00:10:05.977 --> 00:10:06.777
Three.

171
00:10:06.233 --> 00:10:09.951
Bend the U in 2D, then form the crown in 3D:

172
00:10:09.951 --> 00:10:13.701
a layer change at the peak, and the curve of the bore.

173
00:10:14.585 --> 00:10:15.385
Four.

174
00:10:14.897 --> 00:10:20.504
Copper springs back about a degree at a bend, and differently from batch to

175
00:10:20.504 --> 00:10:23.173
batch, so lines overbend, measure and correct.

176
00:10:24.345 --> 00:10:27.126
That is the end of lecture three.

177
00:10:27.126 --> 00:10:28.389
Next, lecture four:

178
00:10:28.389 --> 00:10:32.018
insert, twist, weld, how 96 pins become one winding.

179
00:10:32.018 --> 00:10:36.837
Until then, look at our pins and their crowns in the free Viewer.

180
00:10:37.785 --> 00:10:42.969
Motoreka is a product of UMPSA, developed at EMDLab.

181
00:10:42.969 --> 00:10:45.381
motoreka.my.

